2026-09-28 — diagnostic requests must survive command projection

RED: the two application command adapters discard -Werror=unused-variable
and -pedantic-errors. The small C fixture has one unused-variable case and
one GNU statement-expression case. With the requested options, the fixed
GCC14/Clang19 O0/O2 controls reject all eight compilations; removing only
the diagnostic request yields eight executable controls returning zero.
Before repair the adapters emit eight EvoCC executables returning zero too,
but only by discarding the requested diagnostic contract. Those are false
acceptances, not successful implementations of either warning policy.

GREEN: the shared predicate preserves -W diagnostic requests, -w, -pedantic
and -pedantic-errors in written order. -Wa/-Wl retain separate consumers;
-Wp is already preserved by the preprocessor policy. Unknown warning requests
also reach the driver instead of being silently removed. All eight repaired
commands explicitly refuse the unsupported option and produce no assembly.
The unit cases independently check override order and forwarding boundaries.

All 317 ladder Python tests, targeted Ruff and git diff --check pass.
A read-only inventory of all 1,339 original musl commands finds no omitted
diagnostic request. Ten actual unchanged original commands now explicitly
refuse the first unsupported -Wno-pointer-to-int-cast option; installed objects
are unchanged. This exposes missing driver warning support, not a C regression
or a successful full musl build. B08b remains OPEN for warning implementation
and other output/assembler accounting. Historical 1,271-object results retain
their recorded omissions and are not regraded as faithful-command results.
No Rust/compiler binary, frozen source, kernel input or runtime disk changed.

